========================================
 How to compile the source of LS-align
========================================

In Linux system, you can compile the LS-align source as

>cd ./src

>g++ LSalign.cpp -o LSalign

or

>g++ -static -O3 -ffast-math -lm -o LSalign LSalign.cpp

----------------------------------------
 In Windows system (MinGW-w64 / MSYS2)
----------------------------------------
Tested with MSYS2/MinGW-w64 g++ 14.2.0 on Windows 11.

Open the "MSYS2 UCRT64" shell (or use Git Bash with the same toolchain on
PATH), then:

>cd /d/qlab/LSalign_src/LSalign_src20180916/src

>g++ -std=c++98 -O2 -static -o LSalign.exe LSalign.cpp -lm

If you prefer a dynamically linked executable:

>g++ -std=c++98 -O2 -static-libstdc++ -static-libgcc -o LSalign.exe LSalign.cpp -lm

The output is src\LSalign.exe.

Notes on Windows:

 1. Link the C++ runtime statically (-static, or at least
    -static-libstdc++ -static-libgcc). If dynamic linking of libstdc++ is
    broken on your toolchain (collect2 exits with code 116), the plain
    "g++ LSalign.cpp -o LSalign" command from the Linux section will fail;
    use one of the two commands above instead.

 2. g++ needs a writable temporary directory. When TMP/TEMP/TMPDIR are empty
    (as they are inside a make recipe), g++ falls back to C:\Windows\ and
    fails with a permission error. Export one explicitly:

      MSYS2 / Git Bash:  export TMP=/c/temp TEMP=/c/temp TMPDIR=/c/temp
      cmd.exe:           set TMP=C:\temp && set TEMP=C:\temp && set TMPDIR=C:\temp

 3. Do not use the Visual Studio command prompt unless it is on PATH; cl.exe
    was not tested here. MinGW-w64 is the supported Windows toolchain.

A test suite (unit + CLI) is provided in ./tests; see tests/README.md.
It builds the sources itself with the flags above (including the temp-dir
workaround), and is the quickest way to verify a Windows build:

>cd /d/qlab/LSalign_src/LSalign_src20180916/tests

>make test

========================================
 How to use LS-align
========================================
In Linux system and the right directory, you can run the program in the command window without arguments to obtain a brief instruction

>./LSalign

Here, an example is shown as follows:

>./LSalign ../example/ace_ligands.mol2 ../example/xtal-lig.mol2

----------------------------------------
 In Windows system
----------------------------------------
In cmd.exe, use backslashes and no leading "./" ("./" is a shell syntax
that cmd.exe does not understand). Run from the directory containing the
executable:

>cd D:\qlab\LSalign_src\LSalign_src20180916\src

>LSalign.exe

>LSalign.exe ..\example\ace_ligands.mol2 ..\example\xtal-lig.mol2

>LSalign.exe ..\example\xtal-lig.mol2 ..\example\xtal-lig.mol2 -rf 1

In PowerShell, prefix the executable with ".\":

>.\LSalign.exe ..\example\ace_ligands.mol2 ..\example\xtal-lig.mol2

In MSYS2 / Git Bash, use the POSIX form:

>./LSalign.exe ../example/ace_ligands.mol2 ../example/xtal-lig.mol2

Exit status: 0 on success, 1 on failure (invalid options, unreadable input,
out-of-range -oq/-ot, ...).  Diagnostic messages go to stderr; the result
table is the only thing written to stdout.  The scripted tests parse the
stdout table and check the exit status separately.



====================
Output Format
====================
QEURY_NAME:  the query ligand name (sic: "QUERY" is misspelled in the
             program; the header is kept as-is for compatibility with
             existing parsers)
TEMPL_NAME:  the template ligand name
PC-score8Q:  the alignment score normalized by the atom number of the query
             ligand.  NOTE: this is the internal LS-score (the weighted sum
             of distance, mass and bond-environment terms) divided by Nq.
             It is NOT the PC-score defined in the paper.
PC-score8T:  as above, normalized by the atom number of the template ligand
Pval_PC8Q:   the P-value of PC-score8Q (Gumbel distribution)
Pval_PC8T:   the P-value of PC-score8T (Gumbel distribution)
JaccardR:    the Jaccard ratio of this alignment
RMSD_lb:     the Vina lower-bound RMSD of this alignment
             (NOT the aligned RMSD reported in the paper)
QUERY_SIZE:  the number of query atoms loaded (hydrogens included with -H 1)
TEMPL_SIZE:  the number of template atoms loaded (hydrogens included with -H 1)
             The second header line, "(Without \"H\")" / "(With \"H\")",
             is printed under these two columns and states whether hydrogen
             atoms are included; it follows the -H option.

(Some detail definitions can be found at http://zhanglab.ccmb.med.umich.edu/LS-align/ReportParam.html)

