We thank that Dr.John Kulp helps us to fix the compiled error about the "abs" function, when using the compilers of g++ 8.2.0 and Visual Studio 2017.

====================
 Limitations (by design)
====================
1. No ring-level flexibility: ring conformations (pucker/chair/boat) are
   frozen to the input conformer.  The `*_RING_*` bond types (28 of them) only
   extend the scoring tables; they do not sample ring conformations and they
   are not the BWJS of the paper (see SI Fig. S12).
2. Limited conformational sampling: at most 3 rotatable single bonds (NRS=3)
   are rotated, from a fixed angle set (5 angles by default, 11 with `-md 1`),
   and at most 10 candidate conformers are scored (12 with `-acc 1`).  There is
   only one conformer-generation path (R5 removed the legacy no-arg N=5 one).
3. Deterministic decimation above 5000 conformers: the decimation branch (a
   fixed evenly spaced subset of 5000, reproducible across runs) is no longer
   reachable from the CLI after R5 removed the N=5 no-arg path: with NRS=3 and
   at most 11 detail angles the set never exceeds 11^3 = 1331.  The code is
   kept (the sampler is shared) but a build can only reach it by extending the
   angle set.
4. `-rf 2` (Fragment mode) is not implemented in this build; it is rejected.
5. Input format: mol2 only (PDB/CIF/SDF are not supported).  ATOM records are
   parsed fixed-column first, falling back to whitespace/Tab tokenisation when
   the fixed columns are not parseable; malformed records are rejected with a
   diagnostic.
6. The score is a weighted heuristic (distance, mass and bond-environment
   terms) normalised by ligand size; it is not the PC-score of the paper, and
   the reported `RMSD_lb` is the Vina lower bound, not the aligned RMSD.
7. No optimality guarantee: the search is a heuristic over the sampled
   conformers and sliding windows; the top-scoring alignment is not claimed to
   be the global optimum.

====================
 Implementation notes (extensions not described in the paper)
====================
* Ring bond types (X2).  `getAtomBondTypes` appends `<BASE>_RING_THREE..SIX`
  on top of the six MOL2 base types when a bond belongs to a ring, so
  `junBondInfoScoMtx` sees extra ring-context keys.  This is scoring only:
  ring bonds are never rotated.
* Initial seeds (R2).  `align()` starts the search from four seed matrices.
  Seeds 1 and 2 have the form of `S_mass` and `S_vdw` in the paper's Eq.(7);
  seed 3 is meant to be `S_bond`, and seed 4 is an extension:
  1. mass difference -- `1/(1+(dm/dm0)^2)`, i.e. Eq.(7)'s `S_mass`;
  2. vdW-radius difference -- `1/(1+(dvdw/vdw0)^2)`, i.e. Eq.(7)'s `S_vdw`;
  3. `bondInfoScoMtx` -- bond-length-weighted coordination-number matching with
     a weak bond-type preference (a matching bond type gets a small boost, a
     mismatched one is not penalised).  This is NOT the paper's BWJS (Eq.(4)):
     different bond types at the same length still score 1.0, and a degree-3
     atom with all mismatched types outscores a degree-2 atom with all matched
     types (3.0 > 2.4), i.e. coordination number dominates;
  4. `junBondInfoScoMtx` -- bond type with ring context (see X2 above), also an
     extension beyond the paper.
  Seed 3 is not the paper's BWJS, and seed 4 has no counterpart in the paper:
  no seed uses BWJS as `S_bond`.  All four are heuristic scoring seeds.
* Detail angle set (X3).  `-md 1` appends +-180, +-90 and +-30 degrees to the
  default 5-angle set (0, +-60, +-120), i.e. 11 discrete angles per bond.
* Candidate selection (X4).  `FlexibleLSalign::selectRotamers` keeps up to 10
  candidates by three tiers: RMSD_lb ascending (sn/2), three-point shape
  fingerprint ascending (sn/2), and, with `-acc 1`, two more by rough rigid
  LS-score descending.  These tiers are an implementation choice, not a
  procedure defined in the paper.
* CLI extensions (X5).  `-rf`/`-md`/`-acc`/`-H`/`-d0`/`-oq`/`-ot` are
  extensions with strict range checks; `-rf 2` and out-of-range `-oq`/`-ot`
  are rejected with a non-zero exit status.
